  "exec_summary": [
    "Reviewed across the API-security and mobile-applications domains, this assessment examined Home Assistant — a self-hosted home-automation platform spanning 23 code-grounded repositories — as one trusted, single-process Core sitting behind a host-root-equivalent Supervisor layer and in front of adversary-controlled mobile Companion clients. Both domain packs are squarely applicable: the API-security lens governs the Core REST/WebSocket surface, the privileged Supervisor API, the add-on role model, and the token lifecycle, while the mobile-applications lens governs the iOS and Android clients, their on-device secret stores, the WebView/JS-bridge, deep links, and the push relay. The defining architectural signal is concentration of blast radius: a single trust domain holds the entire secret estate as plaintext on disk, a container-to-host-root bridge accepts privileged operations with no caller identity check, and consequential physical actions leave no attributable record.",
    "The finding shape is a heavy concentration of high-severity trustworthiness and auditability gaps standing against a genuinely real — but narrowly-scoped — set of confirmed controls. The most material weaknesses cluster in three places: at-rest confidentiality and integrity of the secret estate (plaintext .storage and the Recorder history DB, where a single file read forges HS256 tokens and a single file write rewrites the permission policy undetected); the host-root control plane (the os-agent D-Bus and add-on→Supervisor→Docker-socket chain); and auditability, where there is no security audit log at all, so lock/alarm actuation, the auth lifecycle, and host-root operations are unattributable. The attack-path analysis found every declared attacker-position/crown-jewel pair reachable, dominated by direct single-hop compromises with no mitigating control on the path. The most actionable next moves are an at-rest protection program over .storage, the Recorder DB, and backups; a real authorization boundary on the os-agent and Supervisor host-root hops; and the introduction of a single attributable security-audit stream — reviewers should work the headline findings in priority order."
  ],
  "posture_summary": {
    "trustworthiness": "Strong primitives at the verification edge (bcrypt with constant-time login, per-entity READ enforcement, a revocation-aware token chokepoint) sit on top of a plaintext-at-rest secret estate where a single file read forges tokens and a single file write rewrites the permission policy and history undetected, plus adversary-controlled mobile clients with unencrypted on-device token/PII stores and no TLS pinning.",
    "scalability": "Single-host, single-process Core is the intended appliance model and an explicit, documented constraint, but 2000+ unsandboxed integrations and the recorder path share one event loop with no bulkhead or quota, credentials never rotate or expire, and the only redundant copy is a default-unencrypted backup that is the sole recovery path.",
    "auditability": "There is no security audit log anywhere — lock/alarm actuation, the auth lifecycle, and host-root D-Bus operations are unattributable — and the host-root bridge accepts privileged calls with no caller identity, while supply-chain provenance (image signing, SBOM, attestation) is advisory rather than enforced."
  },
