  "contradictions": [
    {
      "id": "contra-067769c7",
      "finding": {
        "id": "intg-4768c0f0",
        "assertion": "Core .storage records have no MAC or content hash, so a directed file-write silently forges refresh-token jwt_keys or escalates a user via the permission policy."
      },
      "capability": {
        "ids": [
          "intg-cap-e18bb24c"
        ],
        "id": "intg-cap-e18bb24c",
        "assertion": "Permission policy compilation fails closed to deny-all on an empty or corrupt policy, so a missing/truncated/unparseable policy denies rather than defaulting open."
      },
      "comparison": "The capability (compile_policy, apply_policy_deny_all) protects against ACCIDENTAL corruption and truncation — a malformed policy degrades to deny — and its own prose explicitly carves out that it does NOT protect against a DIRECTED tamper-to-True/allow-all. The finding is precisely that directed tamper: a file-write that sets policy to True or forges a jwt_key, which fail-closed compilation does not detect or prevent. The two address disjoint threat models (random corruption vs. adversarial write) on the same policy artifact.",
      "resolution": "Keep both. The fail-closed capability is a genuine resilience/safe-default property; it neither covers nor weakens the directed-tamper finding. The MAC/content-hash remediation in the finding is the control that would close the directed-tamper gap the capability self-admits it does not."
    },
    {
      "id": "contra-7dcf3d1e",
      "finding": {
        "id": "intg-5df46115",
        "assertion": "mobile_app webhook_call_service actuates arbitrary services (locks/alarms) with no per-entity write-path authorization, mutating state outside the permission engine."
      },
      "capability": {
        "ids": [
          "intg-cap-ecebb879"
        ],
        "id": "intg-cap-ecebb879",
        "assertion": "Per-entity READ permission is enforced at the state view with deny-all-by-default policy compilation; the code-recon pass resolves the brief's load-bearing authz gap to ENFORCED for reads."
      },
      "comparison": "The capability's scope is explicitly the READ path (APIEntityStateView.get, check_entity POLICY_READ, compile_policy fail-closed) and even names the doc note it resolves; the finding's scope is the webhook write path (webhook_call_service, registration_context, no per-entity check) which routes AROUND the permission engine entirely. The capability never claims write-path or webhook-path coverage, so its ENFORCED-for-reads attestation does not touch the finding's claim.",
      "resolution": "Keep both. The capability resolves the READ-side documentation staleness; the finding's WRITE-side and webhook-bypass gaps are untouched by it and remain open. No maturity change."
    },
    {
      "id": "contra-f45409a0",
      "finding": {
        "id": "intg-d6c5e666",
        "assertion": "POST /api/states write enforces only is_admin with no per-entity write policy, so any admin token mutates arbitrary entity state."
      },
      "capability": {
        "ids": [
          "conf-cap-88473a97"
        ],
        "id": "conf-cap-88473a97",
        "assertion": "APIEntityStateView.get enforces per-entity check_entity(POLICY_READ) and filter_entity_ids_by_permission scopes the readable set per the compiled policy, resolving the brief's open question to ENFORCED for reads."
      },
      "comparison": "The capability cites the GET path (L242-L255, check_entity POLICY_READ) and is explicit that it scopes READS; the finding cites the POST path (L257-L275, is_admin-or-deny) and is explicit that it concerns WRITES. The same permission engine is per-entity on read but coarse admin-or-deny on write — an asymmetry both records independently observe — so they describe adjacent, non-overlapping surfaces rather than the same claim.",
      "resolution": "Keep both: the capability stands as a confirmed READ-path control and the finding stands as an open WRITE-path gap. Neither downgrades the other; the asymmetry itself is the actionable signal (extend per-entity policy to the write path)."
    }
  ],
